      "source": [
        "# 📈 CIR Interest Rate Model\n",
        [![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Proppls/CIR-Model/blob/main/finClub2.ipynb)
        "\n",
        "> **Goal:** Learn how to model and predict the yield curve using the Cox–Ingersoll–Ross (CIR) model.  \n",
        "> By the end of this notebook you will be able to: load & clean bond yield data, calibrate a short-rate model, and evaluate predictions out-of-sample.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🔑 Key Terms\n",
        "\n",
        "| Term | Plain-English Meaning |\n",
        "|------|----------------------|\n",
        "| **Yield** | The annual return you earn if you hold a bond to maturity (expressed as a %) |\n",
        "| **Zero-Coupon Bond** | A bond that pays no coupons; you buy it cheap and get face value at maturity |\n",
        "| **Maturity / Tenor** | How far in the future the bond matures (e.g. 3 months, 2 years, 10 years) |\n",
        "| **Yield Curve** | A snapshot of yields across all maturities on a single day |\n",
        "| **Short Rate $r_t$** | The instantaneous interest rate at time $t$ — the \"seed\" from which the whole yield curve grows |\n",
        "| **Mean Reversion** | The tendency of rates to drift back towards a long-run average $\\theta$ |\n",
        "| **SDE** | Stochastic Differential Equation — describes how a random variable evolves continuously in time |\n",
        "| **Affine Model** | A model where the yield is a *linear* (affine) function of the state variable $r_t$ |\n",
        "| **Calibration** | Finding the model parameters (e.g. $\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma$) that best fit observed market data |\n",
        "| **Out-of-Sample** | Evaluating predictions on data the model has never seen during training |\n",
        "| **Feller Condition** | A mathematical requirement ($2\\kappa\\theta \\geq \\sigma^2$) that guarantees rates stay positive |\n",
        "| **GMM** | Generalised Method of Moments — an optimisation-based calibration approach |\n",
        "| **R²** | Coefficient of determination; $R^2 = 1$ is perfect prediction, $R^2 = 0$ means the model is no better than a constant |\n",
        "| **RMSE** | Root Mean Squared Error — average prediction error in the same units as the yield |\n",
        "| **Basis Point (bps)** | $1$ bps $= 0.01\\%$ — the standard unit for quoting small interest-rate differences |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🗺️ Notebook Roadmap\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Setup** — imports and constants  \n",
        "2. **Data Loading** — load train/test CSVs  \n",
        "3. **Preprocessing** — clean the data, handle gaps and outliers  \n",
        "4. **Theory: CIR Model** — the SDE, bond price formula, and yield formula  \n",
        "5. **CIR Class Definition** — code the model  \n",
        "6. **Calibration** — fit parameters to training data  \n",
        "7. **Yield Curve Example Plot** — visualise what the model produces  \n",
        "8. **Prediction Protocol** — how to invert the 3M yield to get $r_t$  \n",
        "9. **Evaluation** — R², RMSE, bias on the test set  \n",
        "10. **Sanity Check** — manually verify the math for one single data point  \n",
        "11. **Two-Factor Extension** — add a slope factor  \n",
        "12. **Model Comparison** — 1-factor vs 2-factor  \n",
        "13. **Critical Analysis** — what works, what fails and why  \n",
        "14. **Summary Table** — final results\n"
      ]
    },
