      "source": [
        "## 📐 Theory — The CIR Short-Rate Model\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3.1 Why Do We Need a Model?\n",
        "\n",
        "We observe the 3-month yield on a test day. We want to predict the 6-month, 9-month, 1-year, and 2-year yields **without observing them directly**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The key insight: if we have a mathematical model that links all yields to a single hidden variable $r_t$ (the **short rate**), we just need to:\n",
        "1. Infer $r_t$ from the 3M yield.\n",
        "2. Plug $r_t$ into the model to get the other yields.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3.2 The CIR Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE)\n",
        "\n",
        "Cox, Ingersoll & Ross (1985) model the short rate $r_t$ as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$dr_t = \\underbrace{\\kappa(\\theta - r_t)}_\\text{mean-reversion drift} \\, dt + \\underbrace{\\sigma \\sqrt{r_t}}_\\text{volatility} \\, dW_t$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Parameter meanings:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Symbol | Name | Intuition |\n",
        "|--------|------|-----------|\n",
        "| $\\kappa > 0$ | Speed of mean reversion | How strongly rates are pulled back towards $\\theta$ |\n",
        "| $\\theta > 0$ | Long-run mean | Where the short rate \"wants\" to be in the long run |\n",
        "| $\\sigma > 0$ | Volatility coefficient | How noisy/jumpy the short rate is |\n",
        "| $dW_t$ | Brownian motion increment | The random shock at each instant |\n",
        "\n",
        "> 💡 **Intuition:** Think of $r_t$ as a ball on a rubber band. The band is anchored at $\\theta$. When the ball is far from $\\theta$, the band pulls it back strongly (large $\\kappa$). Noise keeps the ball jiggly (large $\\sigma$).\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3.3 The Feller Condition\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\boxed{2\\kappa\\theta \\geq \\sigma^2}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If this holds, the short rate **can never reach zero** — it will always stay strictly positive. This is important because negative interest rates would make bond pricing complicated.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3.4 Closed-Form Bond Price\n",
        "\n",
        "For a zero-coupon bond maturing at time $T$, the price at time $t$ is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P(t, T) = A(\\tau) \\cdot e^{-B(\\tau) \\, r_t}, \\quad \\tau = T - t$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\gamma = \\sqrt{\\kappa^2 + 2\\sigma^2}$ and:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$B(\\tau) = \\frac{2(e^{\\gamma\\tau}-1)}{(\\gamma+\\kappa)(e^{\\gamma\\tau}-1) + 2\\gamma}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$A(\\tau) = \\left[\\frac{2\\gamma \\, e^{(\\kappa+\\gamma)\\tau/2}}{(\\gamma+\\kappa)(e^{\\gamma\\tau}-1)+2\\gamma}\\right]^{\\!2\\kappa\\theta/\\sigma^2}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3.5 The Yield Formula (What We Actually Use)\n",
        "\n",
        "The continuously-compounded yield is just $y = -\\ln P / \\tau$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\boxed{y(\\tau) = \\frac{B(\\tau) \\cdot r_t - \\ln A(\\tau)}{\\tau}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is **affine (linear) in $r_t$**: we can write $y(\\tau) = c_0(\\tau) + c_1(\\tau) \\cdot r_t$, which means:\n",
        "- Given $r_t$, we can instantly compute the yield at any maturity.\n",
        "- Given a yield at one maturity, we can **solve backwards** for $r_t$.\n"
      ]
    },
