      "source": [
        "## ⚙️ Calibration — Fitting the Model to Data\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why Cross-Sectional GMM Instead of MLE?\n",
        "\n",
        "We have two main ways to estimate $\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma$:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | What it fits | Typical $\\sigma$ | Yield-curve $R^2$ |\n",
        "|--------|-------------|------------------|-------------------|\n",
        "| **Cross-sectional GMM** *(our choice)* | Yield curve shape on each date | $\\approx 0$ | High (> 0.85) |\n",
        "| **Time-series MLE** | Day-to-day changes in the short rate | $\\approx 0.04$–$0.10$ | Lower |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why GMM wins here:** Our goal is to predict the yield curve shape. Each trading day gives us 9 yield observations for 3 parameters — that's 9 moment conditions, so the system is **overdetermined and well-identified**. GMM directly minimises the quantity we care about: yield curve fit.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The trade-off:** GMM drives $\\sigma \\to 0$ because the smooth shape of the yield curve is best explained by the deterministic part of the model ($\\kappa, \\theta$). This means the fitted $\\sigma$ does **not** reflect the real volatility of interest rates — it just means the curve's shape on any given day is nearly fully explained by $r_t$, $\\kappa$, and $\\theta$ alone.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Two-Stage Calibration Procedure\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\underbrace{\\text{Stage 1: Grid Search}}_{\\sim 144 \\text{ evaluations}} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; \\underbrace{\\text{Stage 2: Nelder-Mead}}_{\\text{fine-tune to } 10^{-12} \\text{ precision}}$$\n"
      ]
    },
