      "source": [
        "## 🔍 Critical Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.1 Why Does $\\sigma \\approx 0$ After Cross-Sectional Calibration?\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall that $\\sigma$ enters the yield formula only through $\\gamma = \\sqrt{\\kappa^2 + 2\\sigma^2}$.  \n",
        "When $\\sigma \\ll \\kappa$, we get $\\gamma \\approx \\kappa$, and the model collapses to a **deterministic exponential interpolation** between $r_t$ and $\\theta$.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not a bug — it is the optimal solution for fitting smooth yield curves when there is no noise in the objective function. The cross-sectional optimiser exploits this:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{Smooth yield curve} \\implies \\sigma \\to 0 \\text{ to minimise cross-sectional MSE}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.2 Calibration Method Comparison\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | $\\sigma$ | Cross-Sectional $R^2$ | Notes |\n",
        "|--------|----------|----------------------|-------|\n",
        "| Cross-Sectional GMM | $\\approx 0$ | **0.89** ✓ | Fits yield shapes perfectly |\n",
        "| Time-Series MLE | $\\approx 0.04$–$0.10$ | $< 0.85$ ✗ | Captures real volatility but shapes are off |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.3 Mean-Reversion Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\kappa \\approx 0.165 \\implies \\text{Half-life} = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\kappa} \\approx 4.2 \\text{ years}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The short rate takes about **4 years** to close half of its gap from $\\theta$. Our test period spans roughly 2 years of falling rates (from $\\sim$5% towards $\\sim$2.3%), so the model sees rates still on their way back — which is why CIR predicts reasonably well.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.4 Why Is the 2-Year Tenor Hardest?\n",
        "\n",
        "Three factors make the 2Y tenor the most difficult:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Boundary effect:** The 2Y sits between the \"anchored by 3M\" short end and the \"converging to $\\theta$\" long end — both anchors pull in different directions.\n",
        "2. **Inverted curve period:** During 2024-H1, the curve was inverted (3M ≈ 5% > 2Y ≈ 4.2%). CIR predicts 2Y *between* 3M and $\\theta$, but the actual 2Y stayed elevated longer than the model's mean-reversion speed implied.\n",
        "3. **Central bank forward guidance:** Fed guidance kept the 2Y higher than a pure diffusion model would predict — information not captured by CIR.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.5 Model Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Single factor:** Cannot independently move the slope and curvature of the yield curve.\n",
        "2. **Constant parameters:** $\\theta$ is a historical average; in regime-change periods it may be wrong.\n",
        "3. **No jumps:** Sudden FOMC announcements cause rate discontinuities that a continuous diffusion cannot replicate.\n",
        "4. **Physical vs. risk-neutral measure:** We calibrate under the physical ($P$) measure, but bond prices are determined by the risk-neutral ($Q$) measure. The gap is a risk premium that CIR ignores.\n"
      ]
    },
